        </table>
        <div class="gema-prompt-footer">338 of 3,779 total prompt entries</div>
      </div>

      <p>The ruling landed in a legal landscape shaped by two US federal decisions and one prior GEMA case. On 23 June 2025, Judge William Alsup of the Northern District of California ruled in <em>Bartz v. Anthropic</em> that training a large language model on lawfully acquired books was "quintessentially transformative" and constituted fair use under 17 U.S.C. § 107. He denied fair use for a pirated library of over seven million books. The case settled for $1.5 billion — covering over 400,000 books — with final approval granted by Judge Araceli Martínez-Olguín on 22 July 2026.</p>

      <p>Two days after Alsup's ruling, Judge Vince Chhabria of the same court granted partial summary judgment to Meta in <em>Kadrey v. Meta</em>, finding that use of copyrighted books to train the Llama model was fair use on the record before him. But Chhabria attached an explicit caveat: "This ruling does not stand for the proposition that Meta's use of copyrighted materials to train its language models is lawful. It stands only for the proposition that these plaintiffs made the wrong arguments." He stated further: "In many circumstances it will be illegal to copy copyright-protected works to train generative AI models without permission." In neither case did the court find that training-data memorisation produced substantially similar outputs.</p>

      <p>GEMA had already won a first-instance ruling against OpenAI at the same Munich court on 11 November 2025 (case no. 42 O 14139/24). That case is on appeal before the Oberlandesgericht München, with no hearing date disclosed.</p>

      <p>One fact in <em>GEMA v. Suno</em> was undisputed. Suno obtained its training data by ripping audio from YouTube, circumventing Rolling Cipher technical protection measures. Suno did not contest this.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="phantom-wrap">
      <canvas class="gema-suno-training-ruling-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Refraction</strong> — Two identical translucent lenses sit symmetrically. Falling particles pass cleanly through the left, bending gently and continuing their descent. Particles entering the right slow, shift to terracotta, and dissolve into its boundary until it glows dense with trapped material. At peak the heavy lens pulls particles from across the canvas. Snap reset.</p>

    <section class="section" id="ruling">
      <h2><span class="num">02 — What Happened</span>The Ruling</h2>
      <p>According to itmr-legal's analysis of the judgment, the oral hearing on 9 March 2026 included playback of original musical works alongside Suno-generated outputs, plus notation excerpts. The verdict was originally scheduled for 12 June 2026 but postponed to 31 July <span lang="de">"aus dienstlichen Gründen"</span> — for service-related reasons. On that date, the 42nd Civil Chamber, presiding Judge Elke Schwager, ruled predominantly in GEMA's favour. The court's press release, authored by Vorsitzende Richterin Cornelia Kallert in her capacity as press spokesperson, announced four prohibited acts.</p>

      <p>The court prohibited four separate acts: reproduction for training purposes in the United States; reproduction through memorisation in the model in Germany; communication to the public by offering the model; and reproduction and communication to the public through the outputs. The first act was governed by US copyright law. The remaining three were governed by German copyright law. One lawsuit produced a ruling under two legal systems simultaneously.</p>

      <div class="gema-acts-wrap">
        <div class="gema-acts-bar gema-acts-us">
          <div class="gema-acts-num">ACT 1</div>
          <div class="gema-acts-desc">Reproduction for training purposes</div>
          <div class="gema-acts-law">US COPYRIGHT LAW<span>17 U.S.C. § 107</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="gema-acts-divider"><span class="gema-acts-divider-label">jurisdictional split</span></div>
        <div class="gema-acts-bar gema-acts-de">
          <div class="gema-acts-num">ACT 2</div>
          <div class="gema-acts-desc">Reproduction through memorisation in the model</div>
          <div class="gema-acts-law">GERMAN COPYRIGHT LAW<span>§ 16 UrhG</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="gema-acts-bar gema-acts-de">
          <div class="gema-acts-num">ACT 3</div>
          <div class="gema-acts-desc">Communication to the public by offering the model</div>
          <div class="gema-acts-law">GERMAN COPYRIGHT LAW<span>§ 15(2) UrhG</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="gema-acts-bar gema-acts-de">
          <div class="gema-acts-num">ACT 4</div>
          <div class="gema-acts-desc">Reproduction and communication to the public through the outputs</div>
          <div class="gema-acts-law">GERMAN COPYRIGHT LAW<span>§§ 15, 16 UrhG</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="gema-acts-label">One lawsuit. Two legal systems. Four acts.</div>
      </div>

      <p>On memorisation, the court rejected Suno's argument that model weights contained only abstract mathematical patterns rather than memorised training data. According to the judgment, as analysed by itmr-legal, the court stated: <span lang="de">"Die Festlegung in bloßen Wahrscheinlichkeitswerten ist unerheblich"</span> — fixation in mere probability values is immaterial. The works were found to be <span lang="de">"reproduzierbar enthalten"</span> (reproducibly contained) in Suno's model versions v3.5 and v4. Multiple sources note the court used a burden-shifting approach (<em lang="de">Beweiserleichterung</em>), inferring memorisation from output similarity. itmr-legal calls this "the most attack-prone element of the judgment." The full reasoning sits in the unpublished judgment and may be challenged on appeal.</p>

      <p>The court also held that infringing outputs were Suno's information, not the users'. According to the judgment as analysed by itmr-legal: <span lang="de">"Nicht die streitgegenständlichen Prompts, sondern die von der Beklagten betriebenen Modelle bestimmten den konkreten Inhalt der Outputs"</span> — not the prompts at issue but the defendant-operated models determined the concrete content of the outputs. Suno argued that output similarities were "prompt-induced constraints," that users had deliberately engineered memorisation through complex prompt inputs. The court's response, per itmr-legal's analysis: <span lang="de">"schlicht falsch"</span> — simply false. The court held that merely offering the model infringed the right of public communication under the unnamed right of § 15(2) UrhG. GEMA's separate claim under § 19a UrhG (the making-available right) was dismissed: the prompt-repetition requirement — up to 176 identical prompts — negated the "at times of the user's choosing" element.</p>

      <p>The court rejected the Digital Services Act hosting safe harbour for Suno, holding that AI-generated outputs constitute the provider's own information — <span lang="de">"eigene Informationen der Beklagten"</span> — in which the defendant assumes an active role. It held that compliance with the EU AI Act does not constitute a defence to copyright infringement, characterising the AI Act as complementing rather than replacing EU copyright law.</p>

      <p>Damages and disclosure were granted for four of the six works: Atemlos durch die Nacht, Daddy Cool, Rasputin, and Mambo No. 5. The claims for Big in Japan and Forever Young failed on this point due to co-author consent issues.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="mechanism">
      <h2><span class="num">03 — The Mechanism</span>Someone Else's Law</h2>
      <p>How did a German court reach a US company's training acts conducted on US servers? The answer begins with § 131 of the Verwertungsgesellschaftengesetz (VGG) — Germany's Collecting Societies Act. The statute provides that disputes over rights administered by a collecting society must be heard at the court where the infringing act occurred or where the infringer has its general venue. According to analysis of the judgment by Tobias Vossberg at the IP law blog itmr-legal, the court read this provision as <span lang="de">"doppelfunktional"</span> — dual-functional — meaning it conferred not only domestic but also international jurisdiction. That reading let GEMA pull Suno's US-based training acts into a Munich courtroom.</p>

      <p>Jurisdiction determined where the case was heard. It did not determine which law applied. For that, the court turned to the <em lang="de">Schutzlandprinzip</em> — the country-of-protection principle. The court's press release confirms the application of this principle. According to itmr-legal's analysis of the judgment, the court grounded it in Article 8(1) of the Rome II Regulation, which directs that the applicable law follows the country whose territory requires protection. US copyright law applied to training conducted in the US. German copyright law applied to memorisation, offering, and outputs in Germany.</p>

      <p>German courts are not limited to German law. According to itmr-legal's analysis, the court ascertained US copyright law under § 293 of the Zivilprozessordnung (ZPO) — Germany's Code of Civil Procedure — which authorises German courts to determine foreign law by their own investigation, without expert testimony. The court then conducted its own four-factor fair use analysis under 17 U.S.C. § 107.</p>

      <p>The structural implication is precise. A collecting society used its domestic venue rule to assert jurisdiction over training acts conducted in a foreign country. The court then applied that foreign country's own copyright law and reached a different result from the foreign country's own courts — because it was looking at a different record. Neither the AI company nor any other party chose the forum in which American copyright law was interpreted.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="distinction">
      <h2><span class="num">04 — The Distinction</span>Three Records, Three Results</h2>
      <p>The Munich court expressly distinguished the two US rulings. The court's press release stated that <em>Bartz</em> and <em>Kadrey</em> were <span lang="de">"signifikant"</span> different because in those proceedings, training data <span lang="de">"nicht substanziell in Outputs zugänglich gemacht wurden"</span> — was not substantially made accessible in outputs. Here, Suno generated <span lang="de">"nach Eingabe von einfachen und ergebnisoffenen Prompts Outputs … die den Originalwerken wesentlich ähnlich seien"</span> — after entry of simple and open-ended prompts, outputs substantially similar to the originals. The press release uses the subjunctive <span lang="de">"seien,"</span> the convention of indirect speech in German: the press spokesperson rendering the court's finding rather than quoting it directly.</p>

      <p>The court found all four fair use factors weighed against Suno. First, no transformation: the outputs served the same purpose as the originals. Second, musical works occupy the core of copyright protection. Third, complete recordings were used, and Suno showed no necessity for using these specific works. Fourth, the outputs functioned as substitutes — <span lang="de">"Substitutionsgut"</span> — freely accessible and substantially similar to the originals. The unlawful provenance of the training copies was additionally cited: stream-ripping from YouTube circumvented technical protection measures under § 95a(2) UrhG.</p>

      <p>The court rejected the Text and Data Mining exception under § 44b of the German Copyright Act (UrhG) on two independent grounds. The court's press release stated: <span lang="de">"Die Memorisierung der streitgegenständlichen Musikwerke überschreitet eine solche Auswertung und ist daher kein bloßes Text und Data Mining"</span> — the memorisation of the musical works at issue exceeds such analysis and is therefore not mere text and data mining. And access was unlawful, because the Rolling Cipher circumvention defeated an effective technical measure.</p>

      <blockquote class="pull-quote" lang="de">
        Ein Geschäftsmodell, demzufolge sich ein Unternehmen kostenlos am (geistigen) Eigentum Dritter für den eigenen Profit bedienen darf, ist den genannten Rechtsordnungen unbekannt.
        <cite lang="en">A business model allowing a company to help itself free of charge to others' intellectual property for its own profit is unknown to the referenced legal systems. — the judgment, as analysed by itmr-legal</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>The distinction the Munich court drew is factual, not doctrinal. Fair use under 17 U.S.C. § 107 is a fact-intensive inquiry. Judge Alsup in <em>Bartz</em> examined a record where a language model had been trained on lawfully acquired books and where no substantially similar outputs were demonstrated. Judge Chhabria in <em>Kadrey</em> examined a record where the plaintiffs — in his own words — "made the wrong arguments" and failed to show dilution or market harm. Chhabria explicitly cautioned that his ruling "does not stand for the proposition that Meta's use of copyrighted materials to train its language models is lawful." The Munich court examined a record where a music model, trained on stream-ripped audio, produced outputs substantially similar to the originals from simple prompts. Same statute. Different facts. Different results.</p>

